{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-k1-reviewer-fable-48","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:221653880963f91141d5","verdict":"no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope","bounded_statement":null,"root_cause":null,"issue_class":null,"evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description designates colony_id as the independent-unit column and states one row per colony with a single terminal outcome, i.e., no repeated measurement structure.","observed_value":"colony_id is the independent unit; one row = one colony = one trial","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":10,"end_line":12,"quoted_text":"One row is: one honey bee colony, carrying its assigned entrance-reducer design, its October condition, and the single survived-or-died call made at the spring inspection\nIndependent unit column: colony_id\nOne trial is: one row"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":22,"end_line":24,"quoted_text":"The colonies were housed on separate stands and managed independently, and the\noutcome is a terminal one-time score, so each line is one independent unit with\nno repeated measurement behind it."}]},{"description":"The CSV contains exactly 24 data rows with colony_ids HV-01 through HV-24, each appearing exactly once; no independent unit contributes more than one row to the Fisher table.","observed_value":"24 rows, 24 distinct colony_id values, 0 duplicates","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":2,"quoted_text":"HV-01,notched,8,1.2,survived"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":25,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"HV-24,open,10,2.3,died"}]},{"description":"The workflow builds the 2x2 design-by-outcome table directly from the once-per-colony rows and runs a single Fisher's exact test, with no join, expansion, resampling, or per-visit replication that could enter a colony more than once.","observed_value":"table = [[9, 3], [3, 9]]; fisher_exact two-sided p = 0.0391","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":119,"end_line":122,"quoted_text":"    notched = Arm(\"notched\", [r for r in colonies if r[\"entrance_design\"] == \"notched\"])\n    plain = Arm(\"open\", [r for r in colonies if r[\"entrance_design\"] == \"open\"])\n    table = [[notched.lived, notched.lost], [plain.lived, plain.lost]]\n    p_value = fisher_exact(table)[1]"}]},{"description":"The report's selected result claims 24 independent colonies, one row per colony, with counts 9/12 vs 3/12 and p = 0.0391; recounting the raw CSV rows reproduces exactly these counts (notched: 9 survived, 3 died; open: 3 survived, 9 died), and the two-sided Fisher p-value for [[9,3],[3,9]] with fixed margins is approximately 0.0391 (one-tail hypergeometric sum ~0.01957, doubled by symmetry).","observed_value":"reported and recomputed table and p-value agree: [[9,3],[3,9]], p ~ 0.0391","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":32,"end_line":32,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Fisher's exact test on 24 independent colonies (one row per colony) returns a two-sided p-value of 0.0391: 9/12 (0.750) of notched colonies overwintered against 3/12 (0.250) of open colonies."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":15,"end_line":16,"quoted_text":"| notched | 9 | 3 | 12 | 0.750 |\n| open | 3 | 9 | 12 | 0.250 |"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The workflow actively guards against the issue class: it aborts with an error if any colony_id appears on more than one row, so a repeated independent-unit entry could not silently reach the Fisher test.","observed_value":"explicit duplicate colony_id check with SystemExit","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":33,"end_line":35,"quoted_text":"    tags = [row[\"colony_id\"] for row in colonies]\n    if len(set(tags)) != len(tags):\n        raise SystemExit(\"every colony_id must appear on exactly one row\")"}]},{"description":"The data description explicitly states that no colony appears twice and that each colony was opened and scored exactly once, ruling out repeated measurements behind the rows.","observed_value":"one line per colony, single spring survival call","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":5,"end_line":8,"quoted_text":"by coin toss with twelve colonies to each design. Every colony was opened once,\nat the spring inspection, and given a single survival call. Nothing else was\nrecorded after the October assessment, so the file has one line per colony and\nno colony appears twice."}]},{"description":"The report's own reading section correctly characterizes the design as having no within-colony replication, with row count equal to the number of independent units.","observed_value":"report claims and data confirm rows = independent units","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":36,"end_line":37,"quoted_text":"Because each colony is observed once, there is no within-colony replication\nto absorb and the row count equals the number of independent units. The"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to demonstrate the issue class by (1) scanning all 24 CSV rows for duplicate colony_id values (none: HV-01 through HV-24 each appear once); (2) looking for any repeated-measure or per-visit structure in the columns or description that would make rows non-independent despite unique IDs (the outcome is a single terminal spring call, colonies on separate stands); (3) tracing the workflow for any join, concatenation, resampling, or per-event expansion that could feed the same colony into the Fisher table more than once (the table is built once from the deduplicated row list, and a duplicate-ID guard would abort execution); and (4) independently recomputing the 2x2 table ([[9,3],[3,9]]) and the two-sided Fisher exact p-value (~0.0391, from a one-tail hypergeometric sum of ~0.01957 doubled by symmetry of the balanced table), both matching the report. No path by which an independent unit enters the row-independent procedure more than once could be constructed from the supplied files.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}